// tests/harness_bench_compiler.zen — Zen's COMPILER-THROUGHPUT benchmark: what it costs to run the
// compiler itself, in TIME, in PEAK MEMORY, and in ALLOCATION COUNT. Sibling of tests/harness_bench.zen
// (which measures stdlib operations in-process); this one measures the compiler as a SUBPROCESS,
// because that is the only way to see cold-cache work, peak RSS, and the cc(1) it shells out to.
//
//   make -f bootstrap/Makefile bench-compiler          # build + run, one line per case (~90 s)
//   make -f bootstrap/Makefile bench-compiler > a.txt  # …after a change → `diff a.txt b.txt`
//   ZEN_BENCH_REPS=7 make -f bootstrap/Makefile bench-compiler   # a real noise floor (slow)
//
// It is deliberately NOT part of `make harness` and NOT part of `make bench`: it takes ~90 seconds,
// it is noisy, and a noisy number must never gate a merge. There is no baseline file and nothing
// compares against one. It records numbers; humans compare them.
//
// ── WHY THIS EXISTS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Nothing measured the compiler. `--build-self` moved 2.27s → 2.39s on one recent change and that was
// DISCOVERED rather than SEEN. Compiler cost is also the number a user feels first, and it is the one
// that grows silently: `zen check` on a five-line hello makes ~156 000 allocations cold and ~124 warm.
//
// ── COLD vs WARM IS THE WHOLE STORY ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Every row states its cache state, because one number would be a lie. Measured here, same command:
//
//   ./zen check <hello.zen>   cold ≈ 91 000 us / 155 967 allocs      warm ≈ 4 000 us / 124 allocs
//
// a 20x time gap and a 1200x allocation gap. `cold` means ZENC_CACHE_DIR was `rm -rf`'d immediately
// before the timed run — every rep of a cold case gets its own wipe. `warm` means the cache was
// wiped once and primed with exactly ONE identical run, then every rep reused it. `n/a` means the
// verb does not consult the compile cache at all — verified, not assumed: `--build-self` and `emit`
// each leave ZERO entries in a wiped cache dir (see the `# proof` block at the end of a run).
//
// The cache dir is this suite's own scratch (`$ZEN_BENCH_DIR/cache`, default
// /tmp/zen-bench-compiler/cache), never the harness's or the user's — so the state is deliberate and
// wiping it destroys nothing.
//
// NOT controlled: the OS page cache. A cold rep re-reads sources that are already in RAM. Dropping
// the page cache needs root; these numbers are therefore "compile-cache cold, disk warm", which is
// also what a developer's second `zen check` of the day actually experiences.
//
// ── OUTPUT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Fixed-width columns, no timestamps or paths, so two runs diff cleanly. Times are MICROSECONDS —
// one unit for a table spanning 400 us (a warm hello) to 3 200 000 us (a self-build), and no rounding
// of the small rows down to a lie.
//
//   case         which compiler invocation (see THE CASES below for the exact command)
//   cache        cold | warm | n/a  — see above; ALWAYS read the row's cache column
//   reps         how many timed runs (ZEN_BENCH_REPS, default 3)
//   min/med/max_us   wall clock of one whole subprocess, sorted; median is the middle rep
//   spread%      (max - min) * 100 / min — THIS ROW'S OWN NOISE FLOOR, printed every run
//   maxRSS_kB    peak resident set of the subprocess tree, from `/usr/bin/time -f %M` (ONE run)
//   allocs       malloc+calloc+realloc calls, from the repo's LD_PRELOAD counter (ONE run)
//
// ── THE NOISE FLOOR IS A COLUMN, NOT A FOOTNOTE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `spread%` is printed for every row on every run, so a reader never has to take a spread on faith
// from a doc comment. Read it BEFORE reading min_us. MEASURED, reps=3, 16-core dev box, two whole
// runs — one with another agent's build running, one quiet:
//
//   busy run:  most rows 2-8%; warm short rows 15-17%; and TWO blowouts —
//              emit_tree 83% (2.18s, 2.26s, 4.01s) and check_tree/warm 101% (26ms, 29ms, 52ms)
//   quiet run: 0-6% on every multi-second row; 16-23% on the sub-15-ms rows
//
// So WITHIN a run the big rows are tight and the small rows are not. ACROSS runs it splits the same
// way, and this is the number that decides whether a diff means anything:
//
//   build_self  3 203 904 -> 3 229 818 us   (+0.8%)      emit_tree   2 180 432 -> 2 171 509 (-0.4%)
//   check_hello cold 92 816 -> 93 791       (+1.0%)      check_tree  warm 25 977 -> 14 057  (-46%)
//
// The multi-second rows reproduce to ~1% across processes and are worth trusting on their own. The
// warm rows are dominated by process startup and scheduling and moved by 1.8x between two runs of
// the SAME binary on the SAME tree — a warm-row "regression" under ~2x is noise until several runs
// say otherwise. The sibling suite recorded the same class of excursion (1.7x from a background `cc`
// finishing); this suite SHELLS OUT to cc, so it is more exposed, not less. Never act on one run.
//
// `sh_floor` and `zen_startup` are the apparatus's own floor, measured the same way as everything
// else: `/bin/true` through the same popen path, and `./zen --version` (a full 7 MB binary load and
// exit, no compilation). Subtract them before believing a small case did any work — a warm
// `zen check` is only a few hundred microseconds of actual checking on top of process startup.
//
// They are also the RUN'S OWN VALIDITY CHECK, and this is the first thing to read. A third run,
// taken while another agent's build had the box saturated, opened with
//
//   sh_floor    n/a  5     2359     5497     7597    222%
//   zen_startup n/a  5     1326     1545     3956    198%
//
// A `/bin/true` that takes 7.6 ms has measured the machine, not the compiler — and sure enough that
// run read `build_hello` cold at 1 344 103 us against 459 807 on a quiet box (2.9x) and `build_self`
// at 4 322 199 median against 3 265 360 (1.3x). So: if sh_floor's spread% is not small, throw the
// whole run away. Nothing in that run was a compiler regression. (One row held anyway: `emit_tree`
// stayed at 2 144 014 us / 2% — the cleanest signal in the table even under load.)
//
// ── WHAT IS MEASURED, AND WHAT IS cc ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `zen build` = check + codegen + a fork of the SYSTEM C COMPILER. On this box cc is ~82% of a
// whole-tree build, so a gcc upgrade would look exactly like a Zen regression if the two were not
// separated. Two independent separations are printed, and they agree:
//
//   1. the `emit_tree` row — `zen emit` is the full frontend + C emission with NO cc, so
//      (build_tree total − emit_tree) is Zen's cc cost;
//   2. the STAGE SPLIT table — harvested from the compiler's own `--time` clocks (driver.zen's
//      tm_* stamps), which report `cc` as its own line. Nothing here reimplements those stage
//      clocks; the table is parsed out of the stderr the compiler already prints.
//
// Measured: build_tree total 14 099 500 us of which cc is 12 037 500 (85%), leaving 2 062 000 us of
// Zen; the emit_tree row read 2 171 509 us for the same closure. Two independent paths agreeing to
// ~5% — and 85% of the headline `zen build` number belongs to gcc, not to this compiler.
//
// ── THE CASES (why these files) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   sh_floor      /bin/true                        the popen+fork+exec floor of this harness
//   zen_startup   ./zen --version                  compiler process startup, zero compilation
//   check_hello   ./zen check $DIR/hello.zen       5 lines, written by this suite into its own
//                 scratch dir so the corpus is byte-stable forever regardless of repo churn. The
//                 smallest thing a real user does, and it still pulls in std.text.fmt's closure.
//   check_tool    ./zen check tools/fuzz-diff.zen  1 050 lines, the largest file under tools/ — a
//                 real mid-size program over std, and tools/ is quiet enough to stay comparable.
//   check_big     ./zen check src/compiler/resolve.zen   2 586 lines, the largest single source in
//                 the tree. NOTE its closure is most of the compiler: Zen checks the whole import
//                 graph, so this is "big file" only in the sense of the file at the root.
//   check_tree    ./zen check driver.zen           the WHOLE compiler, ~57 000 lines of closure.
//   emit_tree     ./zen emit driver.zen            same closure, plus C emission, minus cc, and it
//                 never touches the compile cache — the cleanest full-compile number in the table.
//   build_hello   ./zen build $DIR/hello.zen       check + codegen + cc on the smallest input.
//   build_self    ./zen --build-self $DIR/seed.c . SEED REGENERATION: the single best
//                 whole-compiler number, and the one that moved 2.27 → 2.39 unnoticed.
//
// A file the size of the old src/compiler/check_validate.zen (~7 700 lines) is no longer in the
// tree — main split those god files apart, and resolve.zen at 2 586 is now the largest.
//
// ── MEASURING ALLOCATIONS AND PEAK MEMORY ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The sibling suite reads glibc `mallinfo2()` in its OWN process. That is unreachable here: the work
// happens in a child. So:
//
//   allocs     the repo's own LD_PRELOAD interposer (scripts/alloc-fuzz/oom-shim.c, ZALLOC_COUNT=1)
//              is compiled into the scratch dir at startup and preloaded into the child, which
//              prints `ZALLOC_TOTAL=<n>` on exit. It counts EVERY malloc/calloc/realloc call — a
//              count, not live bytes, and the complement of the sibling's liveB/op. If `cc` cannot
//              build the shim, this column reads -1 for every row rather than a fabricated number.
//              `build` cases fork cc, which inherits the preload and prints its own total; the
//              parent exits last, so the pipeline takes `tail -1` — the ZEN process's count.
//              The shim roughly doubles wall time, which is why it gets its own single run and is
//              never inside the timed loop. It prints only if it initialised, i.e. only if the child
//              allocated at all — which is why `sh_floor` (/bin/true) honestly reads -1 rather than 0.
//   maxRSS_kB  `/usr/bin/time -f '@RSS %M'`, i.e. getrusage(RUSAGE_CHILDREN).ru_maxrss — the peak
//              of the child tree, in KiB. Not valgrind (30x too slow for a 14-second case) and not
//              mallinfo2 (wrong process). It is a peak, so it does not decompose by stage.
//              Deterministic enough to be worth ONE run; if it ever moves between runs, say so.
//
// Both extra columns come from their own SINGLE run, at the same cache state as the timed loop, and
// are outside the timing. They are not averaged and they are not sampled — they do not need to be:
// unlike wall clock, neither moves with machine load.
//
// ── PROVING THE WORK IS REAL ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A benchmark that measures nothing is worse than none, so every run ends with a `# proof` block
// that re-derives the claims from the filesystem instead of asserting them: the cache dir is counted
// empty after a wipe, counted again after a cold run (1 entry appears), counted again after a warm
// run (no new entry — the same key was reused), and `zen emit` of the whole tree is piped to `wc -c`
// to show it really produced ~3.3 MB of C. If those numbers ever read 0, the table above is fiction.
//
// ── NO BASELINE FILE, NO GATE, ON PURPOSE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Nothing here exits nonzero for a slow number. The process exit status is 0 unless the harness
// itself could not run at all. Benchmarks record; humans compare.
{ put } = std.text.fmt
{ sb } = std.text.sb
{ sort_by } = std.sort
{ Malloc, malloc, free } = std.mem.alloc
{ run } = std.sys.process
{ mono_ns } = std.time.clock
{ len, find, at, is_empty, try_parse_int, view } = std.text.str
{ expect } = std.core.result
{ getEnv } = std.sys.os
{ exists } = std.sys.fs
{ save } = std.io.file
// ── knobs ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The scratch root: cache dir, generated corpus, build outputs and the alloc shim all live under it,
// and it is wiped at startup. Nothing outside it is written or removed.
DEFAULT_DIR = () string_view { "/tmp/zen-bench-compiler" }
DEFAULT_REPS = () i64 { 3 }
// The five-line corpus. Byte-stable by construction: it is this literal, not a repo file, so the
// small-input rows stay comparable across every future edit to examples/ and tests/.
HELLO_SRC = () string_view {
    "{ println } = std.text.fmt\n\nmain* = () i32 {\n    println(\"hello\")\n    0\n}\n"
}
// cache-state tags (the `cache` column). NA = the verb never consults the compile cache.
CACHE_NA = () i64 { 0 }
CACHE_COLD = () i64 { 1 }
CACHE_WARM = () i64 { 2 }
// `""` alone infers the narrower `string_literal`, which nothing computed can be assigned to; going
// through a function pins these at `string_view` (tools/fuzz-mutate.zen uses the same idiom).
cb_empty_sv = () string_view { "" }
g_dir := cb_empty_sv()
g_cache := cb_empty_sv()
g_shim := cb_empty_sv()
g_have_shim := false
// ── report formatting ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Widths mirror between header and row EXACTLY, with no trailing space on the last column, so a run
// has no trailing whitespace anywhere and two runs diff byte-for-byte.
NAME_W = () i64 { 14 }
CACHE_W = () i64 { 7 }
REPS_W = () i64 { 6 }
US_W = () i64 { 12 }
SPREAD_W = () i64 { 9 }
RSS_W = () i64 { 12 }
ALLOC_W = () i64 { 12 }
STAGE_W = () i64 { 11 }
cb_sp = (n: i64) i64 {
    w := to_i64(0)
    i := to_i64(0)
    @while (i < n) {
        w = w + put(" ").n
        i = i + 1
    }
    w
}
cb_digits = (v: i64) i64 {
    d := (v < 0).then({ 2 }, { 1 })
    x := (v < 0).then({ 0 - v }, { v })
    @while (x > 9) {
        d = d + 1
        x = x / 10
    }
    d
}
// right-align `v` in a `w`-wide column. The pad is bound BEFORE the digits are written: `a + b` has
// no defined evaluation order in the emitted C, so one combined expression could print the number
// ahead of its own padding.
cb_num = (v: i64, w: i64) i64 {
    pad := cb_sp(w - cb_digits(v))
    pad + put("").i(v).n
}
cb_text = (t: string_view, w: i64) i64 {
    head := put(t).n
    head + cb_sp(w - len(t))
}
cb_rtext = (t: string_view, w: i64) i64 {
    pad := cb_sp(w - len(t))
    pad + put(t).n
}
cb_cache_name = (c: i64) string_view {
    (c == CACHE_COLD()).match ({
        true  => "cold",
        false => (c == CACHE_WARM()).then({ "warm" }, { "n/a" }),
    })
}
cb_note = (t: string_view) void {
    put(t).n
    { }
}
cb_header = () i64 {
    cb_text("case", NAME_W())
    cb_rtext("cache", CACHE_W() - 1)
    cb_sp(1)
    cb_rtext("reps", REPS_W() - 1)
    cb_sp(1)
    cb_rtext("min_us", US_W() - 1)
    cb_sp(1)
    cb_rtext("med_us", US_W() - 1)
    cb_sp(1)
    cb_rtext("max_us", US_W() - 1)
    cb_sp(1)
    cb_rtext("spread%", SPREAD_W() - 1)
    cb_sp(1)
    cb_rtext("maxRSS_kB", RSS_W() - 1)
    cb_sp(1)
    cb_rtext("allocs", ALLOC_W() - 1)
    put("\n").n
}
// one report row. A -1 in maxRSS_kB or allocs means the measurement was unavailable, never a guess.
cb_row = (
    name: string_view,
    cache: i64,
    reps: i64,
    lo: i64,
    mid: i64,
    hi: i64,
    rss: i64,
    allocs: i64,
) i64 {
    cb_text(name, NAME_W())
    cb_rtext(cb_cache_name(cache), CACHE_W() - 1)
    cb_sp(1)
    cb_num(reps, REPS_W() - 1)
    cb_sp(1)
    cb_num(lo, US_W() - 1)
    cb_sp(1)
    cb_num(mid, US_W() - 1)
    cb_sp(1)
    cb_num(hi, US_W() - 1)
    cb_sp(1)
    cb_num((hi - lo) * 100 / (lo < 1).then({ 1 }, { lo }), SPREAD_W() - 1)
    cb_sp(1)
    cb_num(rss, RSS_W() - 1)
    cb_sp(1)
    cb_num(allocs, ALLOC_W() - 1)
    put("\n").n
}
// ── number scraping ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Read the unsigned integer that follows position `i` (any leading spaces skipped). -1 when there is
// no digit run there — an absent number is reported as absent, never defaulted to 0.
cb_num_from = (s: string_view, start: i64) i64 {
    n := len(s)
    i := start
    @while (i < n && s.at(i) == ' ') {
        i = i + 1
    }
    v := to_i64(0)
    got := false
    @while (i < n && s.at(i) >= '0' && s.at(i) <= '9') {
        v = v * 10 + to_i64(s.at(i) - '0')
        got = true
        i = i + 1
    }
    got.then({ v }, { 0 - 1 })
}
cb_num_after = (s: string_view, marker: string_view) i64 {
    at_i := s.find(marker)
    (at_i < 0).then({
        return 0 - 1
    })
    s.cb_num_from(at_i + len(marker))
}
// Read a `<int>.<frac> ms` stage clock as MICROSECONDS ("42.6 ms" -> 42600). The compiler prints one
// decimal; up to three are consumed so a future widening does not silently truncate.
cb_us_after = (s: string_view, marker: string_view) i64 {
    at_i := s.find(marker)
    (at_i < 0).then({
        return 0 - 1
    })
    n := len(s)
    i := at_i + len(marker)
    @while (i < n && s.at(i) != '\n' && (s.at(i) < '0' || s.at(i) > '9')) {
        // Skip to the first digit ON THIS LINE, not merely past spaces: one label carries a parenthesised
        // qualifier (`other (io/cache)`), and the newline guard keeps a stage that printed no number from
        // silently borrowing the next line's.
        i = i + 1
    }
    ms := to_i64(0)
    got := false
    @while (i < n && s.at(i) >= '0' && s.at(i) <= '9') {
        ms = ms * 10 + to_i64(s.at(i) - '0')
        got = true
        i = i + 1
    }
    (got == false).then({
        return 0 - 1
    })
    frac := to_i64(0)
    w := to_i64(100)
    (i < n && s.at(i) == '.').then({
        i = i + 1
    })
    @while (i < n && w > 0 && s.at(i) >= '0' && s.at(i) <= '9') {
        frac = frac + to_i64(s.at(i) - '0') * w
        w = w / 10
        i = i + 1
    }
    ms * 1000 + frac
}
// ── running subprocesses ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Run `cmd` for effect and throw its captured output away. Used for setup only — never timed.
cb_quiet = (mp: MutPtr<Malloc>, cmd: string_view) void {
    mp.run(cmd).match ({
        .Ok(o)  => free(o.stdout.view().ptr),
        .Err(e) => { },
    })
}
// Run `cmd` and scrape the integer after `marker` from its captured stdout.
cb_scrape = (mp: MutPtr<Malloc>, cmd: string_view, marker: string_view) i64 {
    mp.run(cmd).match ({
        .Err(e) => 0 - 1,
        .Ok(o)  => {
            v := o.stdout.cb_num_after(marker)
            free(o.stdout.view().ptr)
            v
        },
    })
}
// Run `cmd` and return its whole captured stdout — allocator-owned, the caller frees it.
cb_capture = (mp: MutPtr<Malloc>, cmd: string_view) string_view {
    mp.run(cmd).match ({
        .Ok(o)  => o.stdout,
        .Err(e) => "",
    })
}
// ONE timed run: the clock brackets popen/exec/wait of the whole subprocess. The apparatus's own
// share of that is measured, not assumed — see the `sh_floor` and `zen_startup` rows.
cb_time_us = (mp: MutPtr<Malloc>, cmd: string_view) i64 {
    t0 := mono_ns()
    mp.cb_quiet(cmd)
    (mono_ns() - t0) / 1000
}
// ── command construction ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Every child gets ZENC_CACHE_DIR pointed at this suite's scratch cache, so cache state is
// deliberate and the developer's own cache is never touched.
cb_timed_cmd = (mp: MutPtr<Malloc>, bare: string_view) string_view {
    mp
        .sb()
        .s("ZENC_CACHE_DIR=")
        .s(g_cache)
        .s(" ")
        .s(bare)
        .s(" >/dev/null 2>&1")
        .done()
        .expect("bench-compiler: oom")
}
// `/usr/bin/time -f '@RSS %M'` around the same command; the `grep` keeps our marker line and drops
// whatever the compiler wrote to stderr, and `tail -1` keeps the outermost (last-exiting) process.
cb_rss_cmd = (mp: MutPtr<Malloc>, bare: string_view) string_view {
    mp
        .sb()
        .s("/usr/bin/time -f '@RSS %M' sh -c 'ZENC_CACHE_DIR=")
        .s(g_cache)
        .s(" ")
        .s(bare)
        .s(" >/dev/null 2>&1' 2>&1 | grep '^@RSS' | tail -1")
        .done()
        .expect("bench-compiler: oom")
}
// The shim prints ZALLOC_TOTAL on stderr, so stderr goes to the pipe and the child's stdout to
// /dev/null (`2>&1 >/dev/null`, in that order). A forked cc inherits the preload and prints its own
// total first; the zen process exits last, so `tail -1` is the zen count.
cb_alloc_cmd = (mp: MutPtr<Malloc>, bare: string_view) string_view {
    mp
        .sb()
        .s("ZENC_CACHE_DIR=")
        .s(g_cache)
        .s(" ZALLOC_COUNT=1 LD_PRELOAD=")
        .s(g_shim)
        .s(" ")
        .s(bare)
        .s(" 2>&1 >/dev/null | grep '^ZALLOC_TOTAL=' | tail -1")
        .done()
        .expect("bench-compiler: oom")
}
cb_free_str = (s: string_view) void {
    free(s.view().ptr)
    { }
}
cb_wipe = (mp: MutPtr<Malloc>) void {
    cmd := mp
        .sb()
        .s("rm -rf ")
        .s(g_cache)
        .done()
        .expect("bench-compiler: oom")
    mp.cb_quiet(cmd)
    cb_free_str(cmd)
}
// ── one case ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `reps` timed runs at the stated cache state, then ONE run for peak RSS and ONE for the allocation
// count at that same state. Cold wipes the cache before EVERY measured run; warm wipes once and
// primes with exactly one identical run, so "warm" means the same thing in every row.
cb_measure = (
    mp: MutPtr<Malloc>,
    name: string_view,
    bare: string_view,
    cache: i64,
    reps: i64,
) void {
    timed := mp.cb_timed_cmd(bare)
    buf := assert_nonnull(malloc(reps * sizeof(i64)))
    xs: [i64] := buf.slice(reps)
    (cache == CACHE_WARM()).then({
        mp.cb_wipe()
        mp.cb_quiet(timed)
    })
    i := to_i64(0)
    @while (i < reps) {
        (cache == CACHE_COLD()).then({
            mp.cb_wipe()
        })
        xs[i] = mp.cb_time_us(timed)
        i = i + 1
    }
    xs.sort_by((a: i64, b: i64) bool { a < b })
    (cache == CACHE_COLD()).then({
        mp.cb_wipe()
    })
    rss_cmd := mp.cb_rss_cmd(bare)
    rss := mp.cb_scrape(rss_cmd, "@RSS")
    cb_free_str(rss_cmd)
    allocs := g_have_shim.match ({
        true  => {
            (cache == CACHE_COLD()).then({
                mp.cb_wipe()
            })
            alloc_cmd := mp.cb_alloc_cmd(bare)
            v := mp.cb_scrape(alloc_cmd, "ZALLOC_TOTAL=")
            cb_free_str(alloc_cmd)
            v
        },
        false => 0 - 1,
    })
    cb_row(name, cache, reps, xs[0], xs[reps / 2], xs[reps - 1], rss, allocs)
    free(buf)
    cb_free_str(timed)
}
// ── the stage split ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Harvested, not reimplemented: `zen build --time` already prints driver.zen's tm_* clocks to stderr
// after the verb finishes. One run per row (these are the expensive cases and the split is about
// PROPORTION, not precision). A binary-cache hit still prints the table, with every compile stage at
// 0 and only `total` nonzero; a stage line the compiler did not print at all would read -1.
cb_stage_header = () i64 {
    cb_text("stage-case", NAME_W())
    cb_rtext("cache", CACHE_W() - 1)
    cb_sp(1)
    cb_rtext("flatten", STAGE_W() - 1)
    cb_sp(1)
    cb_rtext("parse", STAGE_W() - 1)
    cb_sp(1)
    cb_rtext("resolve", STAGE_W() - 1)
    cb_sp(1)
    cb_rtext("check", STAGE_W() - 1)
    cb_sp(1)
    cb_rtext("emit", STAGE_W() - 1)
    cb_sp(1)
    cb_rtext("cc", STAGE_W() - 1)
    cb_sp(1)
    cb_rtext("other", STAGE_W() - 1)
    cb_sp(1)
    cb_rtext("total", STAGE_W() - 1)
    put("\n").n
}
cb_stage_cell = (out: string_view, marker: string_view) i64 {
    cb_num(out.cb_us_after(marker), STAGE_W() - 1)
    cb_sp(1)
}
cb_stage = (mp: MutPtr<Malloc>, name: string_view, bare: string_view, cache: i64) void {
    (cache == CACHE_WARM()).match ({
        true  => {
            prime := mp.cb_timed_cmd(bare)
            mp.cb_wipe()
            mp.cb_quiet(prime)
            cb_free_str(prime)
        },
        false => mp.cb_wipe(),
    })
    cmd := mp
        .sb()
        .s("ZENC_CACHE_DIR=")
        .s(g_cache)
        .s(" ")
        .s(bare)
        .s(" 2>&1 >/dev/null")
        .done()
        .expect("bench-compiler: oom")
    out := mp.cb_capture(cmd)
    cb_text(name, NAME_W())
    cb_rtext(cb_cache_name(cache), CACHE_W() - 1)
    cb_sp(1)
    out.cb_stage_cell("  flatten ")
    out.cb_stage_cell("  parse ")
    out.cb_stage_cell("  resolve ")
    out.cb_stage_cell("  check ")
    out.cb_stage_cell("  mono+emit ")
    out.cb_stage_cell("  cc ")
    out.cb_stage_cell("  other ")
    cb_num(out.cb_us_after("  total "), STAGE_W() - 1)
    put("\n").n
    cb_free_str(out)
    cb_free_str(cmd)
}
// ── proof block ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Re-derive from the filesystem what the table above claims, on every run.
cb_count_cache = (mp: MutPtr<Malloc>) i64 {
    cmd := mp
        .sb()
        .s("ls -1 ")
        .s(g_cache)
        .s(" 2>/dev/null | wc -l")
        .done()
        .expect("bench-compiler: oom")
    v := mp.cb_scrape(cmd, "")
    cb_free_str(cmd)
    v
}
cb_proof_line = (label: string_view, v: i64) void {
    put("# ")
        .s(label)
        .s(" = ")
        .i(v)
        .nl().n
    { }
}
cb_proof = (mp: MutPtr<Malloc>, hello_check: string_view) void {
    cb_note("# proof the measured work is real (re-derived from the filesystem, this run)\n")
    // The WRAPPED command, exactly what the timed loop ran: a bare one would inherit the ambient
    // ZENC_CACHE_DIR and count entries in a dir it never wrote — which is how this block earned its
    // keep, by reading `0` the first time it ran.
    wrapped := mp.cb_timed_cmd(hello_check)
    mp.cb_wipe()
    cb_proof_line("cache entries after wipe                 (want 0)", mp.cb_count_cache())
    mp.cb_quiet(wrapped)
    cb_proof_line("cache entries after ONE cold check_hello (want 1)", mp.cb_count_cache())
    mp.cb_quiet(wrapped)
    cb_proof_line("cache entries after a warm re-check      (want 1: same key reused)", mp.cb_count_cache())
    cb_free_str(wrapped)
    emit_cmd := mp
        .sb()
        .s("./zen emit driver.zen 2>/dev/null | wc -c")
        .done()
        .expect("bench-compiler: oom")
    cb_proof_line("bytes of C from `zen emit driver.zen`    (want millions)", mp.cb_scrape(emit_cmd, ""))
    cb_free_str(emit_cmd)
    mp.cb_wipe()
    build_self := mp
        .sb()
        .s("ZENC_CACHE_DIR=")
        .s(g_cache)
        .s(" ./zen --build-self ")
        .s(g_dir)
        .s("/seed.c . >/dev/null 2>&1")
        .done()
        .expect("bench-compiler: oom")
    mp.cb_quiet(build_self)
    cb_proof_line("cache entries after --build-self         (want 0: `n/a` is verified)", mp.cb_count_cache())
    cb_free_str(build_self)
}
// ── setup ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A positive integer from the environment, or `dflt` when unset/unparseable/non-positive.
cb_env_n = (name: string_view, dflt: i64) i64 {
    e := getEnv(name)
    is_empty(e).match ({
        true  => dflt,
        false => try_parse_int(e).match ({
            .Ok(v)  => (v < 1).then({ dflt }, { v }),
            .Err(x) => dflt,
        }),
    })
}
cb_env_s = (name: string_view, dflt: string_view) string_view {
    e := getEnv(name)
    is_empty(e).then({ dflt }, { e })
}
cb_join = (mp: MutPtr<Malloc>, a: string_view, b: string_view) string_view {
    mp
        .sb()
        .s(a)
        .s(b)
        .done()
        .expect("bench-compiler: oom")
}
// Compile the repo's LD_PRELOAD allocation counter into the scratch dir. A box without a working
// `cc` still gets every other column; the allocs column then reads -1 on every row.
cb_setup_shim = (mp: MutPtr<Malloc>) void {
    cmd := mp
        .sb()
        .s("cc -shared -fPIC -O2 scripts/alloc-fuzz/oom-shim.c -ldl -o ")
        .s(g_shim)
        .s(" 2>/dev/null")
        .done()
        .expect("bench-compiler: oom")
    mp.cb_quiet(cmd)
    cb_free_str(cmd)
    g_have_shim = exists(g_shim)
    (g_have_shim == false).then({
        cb_note("# WARNING: could not build scripts/alloc-fuzz/oom-shim.c — allocs column is -1\n")
    })
}
cb_setup = (mp: MutPtr<Malloc>) string_view {
    g_dir = cb_env_s("ZEN_BENCH_DIR", DEFAULT_DIR())
    g_cache = mp.cb_join(g_dir, "/cache")
    g_shim = mp.cb_join(g_dir, "/alloc-shim.so")
    reset := mp
        .sb()
        .s("rm -rf ")
        .s(g_dir)
        .s(" && mkdir -p ")
        .s(g_dir)
        .done()
        .expect("bench-compiler: oom")
    mp.cb_quiet(reset)
    cb_free_str(reset)
    hello := mp.cb_join(g_dir, "/hello.zen")
    hello.save(HELLO_SRC(), len(HELLO_SRC())).expect("bench-compiler: cannot write the corpus")
    mp.cb_setup_shim()
    hello
}
main* = () i32 {
    m := Malloc(_: 0)
    mp := m.addr()
    hello := mp.cb_setup()
    reps := cb_env_n("ZEN_BENCH_REPS", DEFAULT_REPS())
    check_hello := mp.cb_join("./zen check ", hello)
    build_hello := mp.cb_join(mp.cb_join("./zen build ", hello), mp.cb_join(" -o ", mp.cb_join(g_dir, "/hello.bin")))
    build_self := mp.cb_join("./zen --build-self ", mp.cb_join(g_dir, "/seed.c ."))
    build_tree := mp.cb_join("./zen build --time driver.zen -o ", mp.cb_join(g_dir, "/zen-next.bin"))
    build_hello_timed := mp.cb_join("./zen build --time ", mp.cb_join(hello, mp.cb_join(" -o ", mp.cb_join(g_dir, "/hello.bin"))))
    cb_header()
    mp.cb_measure("sh_floor", "/bin/true", CACHE_NA(), reps)
    mp.cb_measure("zen_startup", "./zen --version", CACHE_NA(), reps)
    mp.cb_measure("check_hello", check_hello, CACHE_COLD(), reps)
    mp.cb_measure("check_hello", check_hello, CACHE_WARM(), reps)
    mp.cb_measure("check_tool", "./zen check tools/fuzz-diff.zen", CACHE_COLD(), reps)
    mp.cb_measure("check_tool", "./zen check tools/fuzz-diff.zen", CACHE_WARM(), reps)
    mp.cb_measure("check_big", "./zen check src/compiler/resolve.zen", CACHE_COLD(), reps)
    mp.cb_measure("check_big", "./zen check src/compiler/resolve.zen", CACHE_WARM(), reps)
    mp.cb_measure("check_tree", "./zen check driver.zen", CACHE_COLD(), reps)
    mp.cb_measure("check_tree", "./zen check driver.zen", CACHE_WARM(), reps)
    mp.cb_measure("emit_tree", "./zen emit driver.zen", CACHE_NA(), reps)
    mp.cb_measure("build_hello", build_hello, CACHE_COLD(), reps)
    mp.cb_measure("build_hello", build_hello, CACHE_WARM(), reps)
    mp.cb_measure("build_self", build_self, CACHE_NA(), reps)
    cb_note("\n# stage split, MICROSECONDS, harvested from `zen build --time` (driver.zen's own clocks).\n")
    cb_note("# ONE run per row, no reps — read the PROPORTIONS. `cc` is the system C compiler, not zen.\n")
    cb_note("# A warm row is a binary-cache hit: every stage reads 0 because none of them ran.\n")
    cb_stage_header()
    mp.cb_stage("build_hello", build_hello_timed, CACHE_COLD())
    mp.cb_stage("build_hello", build_hello_timed, CACHE_WARM())
    mp.cb_stage("build_tree", build_tree, CACHE_COLD())
    put("\n").n
    mp.cb_proof(check_hello)
    0
}
